WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:09.762
This is Chapter 3: the
migration path matrix as code.

4
00:00:09.762 --> 00:00:14.512
Every service account and app needs a
decision before anything is converted,

5
00:00:14.512 --> 00:00:19.559
and this chapter turns the book's matrix
into reviewed data and code that proposes

6
00:00:19.559 --> 00:00:24.087
a target, checks the owner's choice,
sequences the waves and checks the gate.

7
00:00:24.087 --> 00:00:26.852
We'll start from Learn's
own decision tables,

8
00:00:26.852 --> 00:00:30.999
see what they leave out, then walk
the code, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:30.999 --> 00:00:36.520
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.782
Why does this chapter matter?

11
00:00:41.782 --> 00:00:46.220
Because three different things get blurred
together, and the code keeps them apart.

12
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.183
The matrix proposes a
target for each account,

13
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:53.082
based on what it is, where it runs
and what credential it holds.

14
00:00:53.082 --> 00:00:56.276
The application owner
decides, and may disagree,

15
00:00:56.276 --> 00:00:58.671
as long as the reason is on the record.

16
00:00:58.671 --> 00:01:02.585
And the security officer's gate
checks that every account has both,

17
00:01:02.585 --> 00:01:05.645
that the riskiest go first,
and that there's a way back.

18
00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:09.908
Treat a proposal as a decision and
you convert accounts nobody agreed to.

19
00:01:09.908 --> 00:01:12.800
First, which cloud this runs against.

20
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:17.450
Which cloud?

21
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:20.517
Most of this chapter never
touches a cloud at all:

22
00:01:20.517 --> 00:01:24.963
the matrix is offline logic over the
files from the first two chapters.

23
00:01:24.963 --> 00:01:28.530
It makes just two reads,
both to Microsoft Graph,

24
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:33.651
and for a GCC tenant like this
estate's, that's the worldwide endpoint.

25
00:01:33.651 --> 00:01:36.325
The higher government clouds
have their own hosts.

26
00:01:36.325 --> 00:01:38.928
One open question is the licensing trial:

27
00:01:38.928 --> 00:01:42.908
Learn says there's no free trial
in the Azure Government clouds,

28
00:01:42.908 --> 00:01:45.893
but a GCC tenant signs in
to the worldwide cloud,

29
00:01:45.893 --> 00:01:49.950
so the chapter leaves it as something
to check in the test tenant.

30
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.100
Now, what Microsoft gives you.

31
00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.719
Here's what Microsoft gives you,

32
00:01:57.719 --> 00:02:00.738
and its order of preference
is one the matrix keeps.

33
00:02:00.738 --> 00:02:04.772
On premises, a group managed
service account whenever possible,

34
00:02:04.772 --> 00:02:07.588
and a plain user account
only as a last resort.

35
00:02:07.588 --> 00:02:12.557
In the cloud, a managed identity if the
service runs in Azure and supports one,

36
00:02:12.557 --> 00:02:15.950
a service principal otherwise,
never a user account.

37
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.730
Windows Server adds the delegated managed
service account, which has real limits:

38
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:25.300
you can't move a managed service account
onto one, and delegation stops working.

39
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.900
Learn turns this into two
decision tables, but no code.

40
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.961
So the repository pins
the pages unmodified,

41
00:02:31.961 --> 00:02:36.610
and a small harness evaluates
each table exactly as published.

42
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.248
This is the heart of the
chapter, in two parts.

43
00:02:43.248 --> 00:02:45.498
Learn's ordering is kept as the model.

44
00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:49.786
But the on-premises table has no column
for the delegated managed service

45
00:02:49.786 --> 00:02:53.723
account at all, so the best target on
a new domain never comes out of it.

46
00:02:53.723 --> 00:02:56.849
It admits a plain user
account in every situation,

47
00:02:56.849 --> 00:02:59.060
so it never tells you to move off one.

48
00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.598
It has no way to say retire,

49
00:03:00.598 --> 00:03:04.698
so an orphaned or disabled account
would just get a new account type.

50
00:03:04.698 --> 00:03:08.710
And it decides on facts like server
counts that no register holds.

51
00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:12.720
The code fills each gap,
and a test pins every row.

52
00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.232
The cloud side has gaps too.

53
00:03:18.232 --> 00:03:22.215
The table recommends a managed
identity if the service supports one,

54
00:03:22.215 --> 00:03:26.757
but leaves you to find out whether it does,
so the code makes that a required fact.

55
00:03:26.757 --> 00:03:30.568
It offers a service principal
only certificates and secrets,

56
00:03:30.568 --> 00:03:33.258
missing federation,
which the book prefers.

57
00:03:33.258 --> 00:03:37.892
It happily recommends a service principal
for a multitenant app, without saying that

58
00:03:37.892 --> 00:03:42.332
Conditional Access can't cover one, so you
could license something and buy nothing.

59
00:03:42.332 --> 00:03:44.433
And nothing records a decision at all.

60
00:03:44.433 --> 00:03:49.207
The verdict: Learn's tables define
preference, but they aren't the matrix.

61
00:03:49.207 --> 00:03:50.780
Let's look at the code.

62
00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:55.855
So what does the code add?

63
00:03:55.855 --> 00:03:59.845
One module and an entry script,
built on the module from Chapter 2,

64
00:03:59.845 --> 00:04:02.705
and changing nothing in
the earlier chapters.

65
00:04:02.705 --> 00:04:05.505
What matters most is who keeps each input.

66
00:04:05.505 --> 00:04:08.907
The matrix itself belongs
to identity engineering

67
00:04:08.907 --> 00:04:11.755
and changes only in a
reviewed pull request.

68
00:04:11.755 --> 00:04:14.155
The application owners keep two files:

69
00:04:14.155 --> 00:04:18.130
where each workload runs, and what
they decided for each account.

70
00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.315
The waves, the program's dates and the

71
00:04:20.315 --> 00:04:23.155
licensing decision each
have their own keeper.

72
00:04:23.155 --> 00:04:27.025
And the earlier chapters' outputs
are refused if their hashes don't

73
00:04:27.025 --> 00:04:31.670
match the manifests, so nobody decides
on evidence the reviewer never saw.

74
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.057
Here's how a proposal is made.

75
00:04:37.057 --> 00:04:41.189
First, scope: some accounts belong
to another plan, some are covered by

76
00:04:41.189 --> 00:04:45.683
their app registration, and some are
out of scope with a reason written down.

77
00:04:45.683 --> 00:04:50.312
Then retirement comes first: if
Chapter 2 found an account unowned,

78
00:04:50.312 --> 00:04:53.932
or it's already disabled,
the proposal is to retire it.

79
00:04:53.932 --> 00:04:57.791
Otherwise, the first matrix row
that matches the account's type,

80
00:04:57.791 --> 00:05:01.057
where it runs and its
credential proposes the target.

81
00:05:01.057 --> 00:05:05.104
And each proposal carries Learn's
cautions, like the fact that an Arc

82
00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:09.940
server's managed identity is shared by
every app its administrators allow.

83
00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.011
The decision check reads
each owner's row and turns

84
00:05:17.011 --> 00:05:20.490
what's missing or wrong into
findings with stable IDs.

85
00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.340
In plain terms: every
account needs a decision;

86
00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:28.440
every account needs enough facts for a
proposal; the target has to fit the decision,

87
00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.590
and Learn's delegated
account limits are enforced;

88
00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.665
an owner who departs from
the matrix has to say why;

89
00:05:34.665 --> 00:05:36.615
anything kept needs an end date,

90
00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:41.340
and anything kept on Kerberos needs an
owner for its service principal name;

91
00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.715
high-risk accounts go first;
every wave needs a way back;

92
00:05:44.715 --> 00:05:47.265
and the licensing scope has to make sense.

93
00:05:47.265 --> 00:05:50.010
The exact wording is on the slide.

94
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.724
A conversion with no way back turns
a failed change into an outage,

95
00:05:57.724 --> 00:06:00.660
so the waves get as much
attention as the targets.

96
00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.096
Every rollback owner and service
principal name owner is looked up once,

97
00:06:05.096 --> 00:06:09.310
and counts only if they're an enabled
member, the same rule as Chapter 2.

98
00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:12.414
An account the owner didn't
place in a wave is proposed

99
00:06:12.414 --> 00:06:15.660
for the first wave if it's
high-risk, the second otherwise.

100
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:19.289
And each wave's rollback is
assembled from its members' targets,

101
00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:22.847
including Learn's own undo for
a delegated account migration,

102
00:06:22.847 --> 00:06:26.220
which keeps the old account
rather than deleting it.

103
00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:34.270
The licensing decision belongs to the
chief information security officer,

104
00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.045
and the code makes it checkable.

105
00:06:36.045 --> 00:06:40.192
Premium adds things like Conditional
Access for workload identities,

106
00:06:40.192 --> 00:06:43.425
but that only covers
single-tenant service principals:

107
00:06:43.425 --> 00:06:46.307
not managed identities,
and not multitenant apps.

108
00:06:46.307 --> 00:06:50.747
So the code works out who could be covered
today and who will leave or join after

109
00:06:50.747 --> 00:06:53.616
conversion, reads the
tenant's subscriptions once,

110
00:06:53.616 --> 00:06:56.007
and checks the decision file against both.

111
00:06:56.007 --> 00:06:58.232
It never buys or assigns anything.

112
00:06:58.232 --> 00:07:01.450
Then the gate check reports
Gate 2's five criteria,

113
00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:04.432
and the gate is met only
when no finding is open.

114
00:07:04.432 --> 00:07:05.880
Who's involved?

115
00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:10.618
Who's this for?

116
00:07:10.618 --> 00:07:13.068
Developers build the decision records.

117
00:07:13.068 --> 00:07:17.304
Identity engineers propose the
targets and keep the matrix itself.

118
00:07:17.304 --> 00:07:20.042
The application owners make the real call,

119
00:07:20.042 --> 00:07:23.405
account by account, and say
where each workload runs.

120
00:07:23.405 --> 00:07:27.386
The chief information security
officer decides the licensing scope,

121
00:07:27.386 --> 00:07:30.680
and the security officer for
the system approves the gate.

122
00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:35.087
As for the tenant, the code asks for
very little: two read permissions,

123
00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:40.650
one to look up the owners and one to read
the subscriptions, and it only ever reads.

124
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.275
Lab 1 needs only a laptop with
PowerShell 7 and Pester, and no tenant.

125
00:07:49.275 --> 00:07:53.524
First you run Learn's tables exactly
as published, and one test makes

126
00:07:53.524 --> 00:07:57.925
the point plainly: the on-premises
table never rules out a user account.

127
00:07:57.925 --> 00:08:02.650
Then you list what the task needs, two
read permissions and nothing but reads.

128
00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:07.150
Then you run the first two chapters and
the matrix over the fictional estate.

129
00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:11.345
The summary tells you how many
identities are in scope and proposed,

130
00:08:11.345 --> 00:08:14.941
how many owners have decided,
and that the gate isn't met yet,

131
00:08:14.941 --> 00:08:17.840
which is exactly what
we want to read next.

132
00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.327
Reading the output is
where the rules turn real.

133
00:08:24.327 --> 00:08:26.883
Six accounts are proposed for retirement,

134
00:08:26.883 --> 00:08:29.803
the orphans and the disabled
ones from Chapter 2.

135
00:08:29.803 --> 00:08:33.751
Others go to delegated or group
managed accounts, some are kept,

136
00:08:33.751 --> 00:08:37.852
and one app has no proposal because
nobody recorded where it runs.

137
00:08:37.852 --> 00:08:41.809
The findings tell the story: a
high-risk account scheduled too late,

138
00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:45.910
a Kerberos account kept with no
owner for its service principal name,

139
00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:48.860
an owner who overrode the
matrix but gave a reason,

140
00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:54.120
and two apps on the licensing list that
Conditional Access can't actually protect.

141
00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.170
Next, the waves.

142
00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.745
The first wave has a named rollback
owner and a clear way back.

143
00:09:02.745 --> 00:09:07.657
The second has an empty owner, which is
exactly why it drew a finding a moment ago.

144
00:09:07.657 --> 00:09:12.649
Then the licensing scope: six apps could
be covered by Conditional Access today,

145
00:09:12.649 --> 00:09:14.616
but only four after conversion,

146
00:09:14.616 --> 00:09:18.246
because one moves to a managed
identity and one is retired.

147
00:09:18.246 --> 00:09:21.557
That's the kind of thing you'd want
to know before buying licenses.

148
00:09:21.557 --> 00:09:26.107
Finally, run the whole suite:
five hundred thirteen tests pass,

149
00:09:26.107 --> 00:09:29.850
and the run made just
three requests, all reads.

150
00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.894
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant
with fictional people,

151
00:09:36.894 --> 00:09:39.625
never production, and it still only reads.

152
00:09:39.625 --> 00:09:42.069
You grant the same two read permissions,

153
00:09:42.069 --> 00:09:45.625
write a small set of inputs for
the test accounts, and run it.

154
00:09:45.625 --> 00:09:49.985
Then come two checks the chapter
couldn't settle from the documentation:

155
00:09:49.985 --> 00:09:54.709
what the license read actually shows in
a GCC tenant, including whether a trial

156
00:09:54.709 --> 00:09:59.650
appears, and which identities the Conditional
Access screen really lets you pick.

157
00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:04.350
Record both, discard the draft
policy, and back everything out.

158
00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.575
Each of these dangers
has a guard in the code.

159
00:10:10.575 --> 00:10:15.312
The biggest is treating the matrix's
proposal as the owner's decision, so they

160
00:10:15.312 --> 00:10:20.125
live in separate files, and the check
flags every account until its owner signs.

161
00:10:20.125 --> 00:10:23.550
A guess about where a workload
runs gets no proposal at all.

162
00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:27.575
Learn's limits on delegated accounts
are enforced, not just noted.

163
00:10:27.575 --> 00:10:31.913
Kerberos accounts can't be kept
forever, high-risk accounts can't wait,

164
00:10:31.913 --> 00:10:34.425
and no wave goes ahead without a way back.

165
00:10:34.425 --> 00:10:38.144
And this code never deletes
anything: retirement still goes

166
00:10:38.144 --> 00:10:42.450
through Chapter 2's rule and the
reviewed decommission process.

167
00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:47.550
Here's what done looks like.

168
00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:52.087
Every account has a proposal, or a
finding that names the missing fact.

169
00:10:52.087 --> 00:10:54.385
Every account has its owner's decision,

170
00:10:54.385 --> 00:10:57.113
with any departure from
the matrix explained.

171
00:10:57.113 --> 00:10:58.944
Nothing kept lives forever,

172
00:10:58.944 --> 00:11:02.925
and every Kerberos keep has an
owner and an accepted end date.

173
00:11:02.925 --> 00:11:06.342
The high-risk accounts go first,
every wave has a way back,

174
00:11:06.342 --> 00:11:10.725
and the licensing scope holds only
identities it can actually protect.

175
00:11:10.725 --> 00:11:12.474
With every account decided,

176
00:11:12.474 --> 00:11:17.037
Chapter 4 moves on to app management
policies and certificate rotation.

177
00:11:17.037 --> 00:11:18.190
Thanks for watching.
